hat I have spent a couple of years  as a creative director here at blind I  started to reach a full stride where I  became very confident in my  communication skills and my ability to  pitch and win work and all of the stuff  that I was worried about in the earlier  part of my career as a creative director  that was off of my shoulders the weight  of having to know what to say and to  sound intelligent and how to speak to  clients and to people all that stuff was  behind me now I felt fully confident and  that was out of my way and the next  couple of years  starting from around 2014 and on I  really got to focus on my craft on my  skills and pushing the work that I was  doing to the next level so I wanted to  share just a few pieces that I'm very  proud of in this period of my life so it  now it's 2014 now comes along this  opportunity where we had some ties to  the band Coldplay we had a connection  over there and we knew as a group as a  company that we wanted to do a marketing  piece meaning something that is highly  visible with a big artist and we usually  did this about one  a year where we're taking on a project  that we knew is going to be an  investment for us of time and energy not  necessarily a profit maker but something  that will be a marketing piece that will  be a calling card for the company and  we've done this over and over again back  in 2006 and 7 it was the music videos  for Gnarls Barkley and we did the crazy  video in 2007 it was the Justin  Timberlake video loved stoned  we're always highly visible got a lot of  traction and pointed a lot of fingers  back at the company so that we can be in  the conversations that we need to be and  get asked to do more pitches or get  asked to do more work 2014 it was my  turn to contribute the challenging thing  with an interactive music video is that  you're not just making one linear video  you're kind of making multiple videos  and the way that I had done it just to  make a little bit more sense for me as  you build it out into three different  tracks there would be the middle track  which is the closest to reality so this  person is going through the world and it  feels the closest to the reality if you  move up a track and track a this is the  most aspirational one where he gets very  things get very dreamy things are very  soft and beautiful and warm and it feels  like this characters long-lost love is  actually there she's still there that  you can chase her and find her and then  track see the bottom is the dark  depressing version so all the colors  were a lot more muted or a lot more cool  and uninviting and as the viewer you get  to pick and go through the sequence do  you want to follow the dark path or the  right path ultimately you get to dictate  how the music video unfolds what story  happens based off of your interaction so  the idea behind this is that because you  have an interactive music video people  will watch it more than once people will  go and watch it the first time and then  try a different path the next time and a  different path the third time actually  did a TED talk about my experience  working on this project specifically and  you'll see me jumping in headfirst into  the deep end not knowing  what I'm knowing and coming out all  right coming out outside of my comfort  zone so I did this TED talk it was  called start before you're ready and  I'll link to that down below if you want  the full story of what happened on this  Coldplay Inc project if you guys don't  know I love video games I've been  playing video games since I was a young  boy and in my teenage years and even  into my college years some days I would  spend 10 hours playing video games like  that's how obsessed I was that's how  much I was into it and all of my life  has been a lot of video games and now  comes this opportunity where Xbox comes  along and this was a huge project so  here was this opportunity Eisenberg had  dropped this in our lap but they said  you know what we like your work we heard  you guys were good  we had never worked with him at this  point but we're putting you against  another company and this other company  was huge it was a company that I admired  for a very long time and I knew they had  over a hundred employees at the time  blind was only about 20 employees so  here it's a David and Goliath situation  where we're going against the heavy  hitters in the industry and here's me  pitching on this thing but the thing is  like I really love Xbox I spent most of  my time playing games on this platform  and so I had looked at the material I  looked at the brief and I started  working with my designers and we crafted  this treatment we submitted the  treatment but before I did on the last  page I wrote this very a heartfelt thank  you and what Xbox means to me and what  it means for me to be a gamer on this  platform and at the end of it I signed  it with my gamertag and a few days later  after submitting the pitch they said you  guys got this you guys won the pitch  and the reason why is because we could  tell that you guys are real gamers so  that passion all those many many years  of playing video games they were not a  waste they came together at this one  point and they served me in this moment  because I was feeling authentically  myself I was showing them that shoot I'm  a gamer I'm just not a creative director  only I'm a guy who grew up on video  games and specifically these characters  the characters that matter  so this premiered at e3 and got a really  good reception they're both from the  audience that was present and online we  won a couple of Clio awards from it and  that was fantastic and I remember  getting a message from the creative  director at the time and he said you  know this is some of the best work that  we've ever done Wow and from that point  forward we became their go-to studio for  everything Xbox so for the next four  years we did everything Xbox  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  since I became a creative director in  2011 as a 26 year old young guy being  not confident speaking to my team to  clients and understanding how to  articulate my thoughts to this moment  here where I'm fully confident in my  skills and able to put it all on screen  use all of the technical things that  I've learned all the design and  communication skills that have acquired  along the way to put out this and then  to have the public react to it the way  that they did  [Applause]  these videos got millions and millions  of views I think over ten millions views  man that was it that was me feeling like  wow I I did something significant as a  creative professional and this was a  nice moment nice time in my life to  celebrate and that was the that was 2017  and yeah I've been so privileged to have  such a good life such good experiences  and great people around me who have  supported me throughout this journey  from my parents and relatives who bought  me art supplies when I was growing up  teachers who to really push me and see  something inside me to tell me I could  do more and be more and to try harder to  having people challenge me and say is  this really your best because you can do  better and then having to go through the  ups and downs of wins and failures and  wins and failures and having the shame  of closing your company and kind of  falling short of all the expectations of  you all of these things have added up  and I'll tell you right now when I was  13 years old or even younger I would  have never imagined I'd be at this point  today I never could have pointed and  said that's where I'm gonna be that's  the job that I'm going to do I didn't  know what every step of their just  leading with curiosity I was just trying  to see what interests me and go deep  until it was dry and then I moved on to  the next thing and then the next thing  and I just kept developing I kept  collecting skills and knowledge meeting  people and just trying to give full  genuine efforts whenever I could so I  share this with all of you guys because  I want you to see that if you go back to  the very beginning you could see my  really bad self-portraits my  disproportionate human characters of my  classmates that I would draw it weren't  that great my Photoshop compositions  didn't make any sense I was  uncomfortable with a lot of mediums and  I was shy and introverted and many times  throughout my career I've been tested  and challenged  but I've been able to figure it out I'm  not any more special than you are  you have all of the same abilities that  I have if you just do the work if you  just show up and if you learn from  failures and just keep pushing forward I  think all of us have these great  opportunities in front of us where if  you look at the gap in front of you and  say this is the thing that's getting in  the way I want you to reframe it and say  this is an opportunity to close I can go  from where I'm at now to where I want to  be and if you can see that as an  opportunity instead of an obstacle and I  think you could really accomplish  anything throughout your creative life  throughout your creative career and for  me even though I'm saying this is the  past 20 years of Who I am I'm not done  I'm still a work in progress and I  haven't fully arrived yet in fact I've  been exploring a lot more personal work  in that past 2 or 3 years things that  are a lot more expressive that has  nothing to do with the client work that  I do but now I'm getting into this  introspective mode where I'm  understanding my thoughts and feelings  I'm starting to put it out there as  words and videos like this one and  pieces of art things that represent Who  I am  and I think I'm starting a new chapter  in my life that's all about artistic  expression and I'm very excited it's  very scary and you can ask anybody here  that I get a little insecure still I'm  not fully confident all the time because  I'm always in a new place in a new  territory I'm 